"Contracts is organised around one question, what makes a promise a contract, so the map is built as a core with an orbit. The four formation subjects (offer, acceptance, consideration, assent) form a tight 2x2 block at the centre, entered from 'Start here' (Commitments & governing law) directly above it. Everything else is placed by its relation to that core and by the ribbons it shares with it. West of the core, level with consideration, sit the two substitutes for a bargain (reliance and restitution; formalities), read as 'or' alternatives. East of the core, level with assent, sits the content of the bargain (terms, interpretation), and beyond it, at the eastern edge, the boundary of the bargain (unfinished deals; terms the law refuses). Third parties float alone in the top-right corner, literally beyond the parties. A horizontal moat of empty space separates 'forming' above from 'the contract in force' below: performance, modification and policing, and excuses run under the core, and the costs of breach (remedies, measuring relief) close the loop at the bottom-left under reliance, whose protected-interest cases they share. Reading order is a clockwise loop, start to core to content to force to cost; the heaviest connection pairs (interpretation-negotiation 17, assent-interpretation 16, assent-terms 15, offer-acceptance 12, remedies-depth 11) are all adjacent. The semester workbench is tucked in the top-left margin outside every zone."